2. Take turns walking downhill and uphill (alternating gradient descent–ascent)
How do the shopper and the referee find the saddle point? They feel which way the ground slopes and take small steps.
The gradient is the slope: it says which direction is uphill and how steep it is. For the flour bill the slopes are
\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x} = 2 - y, \qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial y} = 3 - x
\]
These say something sensible:

If the fine \(y\) is less than $2, flour costs more than the fine it saves you, so the shopper wants to buy less.
If \(x\) is less than 3, the shopper is short of flour, so the referee wants to make the fine bigger.

For any linear program the slopes are \(c + A^\top y\) for the shopper and \(Ax - b\) for the referee.
The shopper walks downhill (makes the bill smaller) and the referee walks uphill (makes the bill bigger). They take turns, and the referee always reacts to the shopper’s newest choice. That is why it is called alternating:
\[
x_{\text{new}} = \text{clip}\Big(x - \tau\,\big(c + A^\top y\big)\Big)
\]
\[
y_{\text{new}} = \max\Big(0,\; y + \sigma\,\big(A x_{\text{new}} - b\big)\Big)
\]
Here \(\tau\) (tau) and \(\sigma\) (sigma) are step sizes: how far each player moves per turn. “clip” pushes \(x\) back inside its limits, and \(\max(0, \cdot)\) stops fines going negative.
Each turn only needs two multiplications by the big table of rules: one by \(A\) and one by its flipped version \(A^\top\). That is cheap even when \(A\) has millions of rows, and graphics cards (GPUs) are very good at it. Methods that only use slopes like this are called first-order methods.

Try it
Drag the sliders and watch the shopper and referee move. Each blue dot is one turn. Both start at zero.

function simulate(eta, n) {
  let x = 0, y = 0, sx = 0, sy = 0;
  const pts = [];
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= n; i++) {
    x = Math.max(0, x - eta * (2 - y));   // shopper walks downhill
    y = Math.max(0, y + eta * (3 - x));   // referee walks uphill
    sx += x; sy += y;
    pts.push({i, x, y, ax: sx / i, ay: sy / i});
  }
  return pts;
}







function saddlePlot(pts, showAvg) {
  const marks = [
    Plot.line(pts, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#4c78a8", strokeOpacity: 0.45}),
    Plot.dot(pts, {x: "x", y: "y", r: 2.5, fill: "#4c78a8"})
  ];
  if (showAvg) {
    marks.push(Plot.line(pts, {x: "ax", y: "ay", stroke: "#f58518", strokeWidth: 2.5}));
    marks.push(Plot.dot(pts.slice(-1), {x: "ax", y: "ay", r: 6, fill: "#f58518"}));
  }
  marks.push(Plot.dot([{x: 3, y: 2}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 7, fill: "#e45756", stroke: "white"}));
  marks.push(Plot.text([{x: 3, y: 2}], {x: "x", y: "y", text: d =&gt; "saddle point (3, 2)", dy: -14}));
  return Plot.plot({
    width: 520,
    height: 420,
    grid: true,
    x: {domain: [0, 6.5], label: "x = kilos of flour (shopper) →"},
    y: {domain: [0, 5.5], label: "↑ y = fine per missing kilo (referee)"},
    marks
  });
}







viewof eta1 = Inputs.range([0.05, 1.2], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Step size"})
viewof n1 = Inputs.range([5, 300], {value: 60, step: 5, label: "Number of turns"})
saddlePlot(simulate(eta1, n1), false)






















Did you spot the problem? They go round in circles! The shopper reacts to the referee, the referee reacts to the shopper, and they chase each other around the saddle point like two kids running around a table. Neither ever catches the other. Smaller steps make smoother circles, but they are still circles.
The good news is in the picture too: the circles go around the answer.

5. Restarts: when in doubt, start fresh
Remember the two problems with averaging? It is slow, and it never forgets the bad early guesses.
The fix is the restart. Once the average has got a lot closer to the answer, Jaddle jumps both players to the average, throws away all the history, and starts a fresh average from there.
Why does that help so much? The size of the circle the players run around depends on how far from the answer they start. Start closer and the next circle is smaller, so the next average is closer still, and so on. Each restart shrinks the error by a fixed fraction. “Ten times more turns for ten times more accuracy” turns into “a few more restarts for each extra digit of accuracy”.

When to restart
Jaddle doesn’t know the answer (if it did, it could stop!), so it can’t measure the distance to it. Instead it uses a wrongness score made from three checks. The score is the largest of them:

Broken rules. How badly does the shopper break the rules? For flour: \(\max(0,\ 3 - x)\).
Unfair fines. Could these fines ever be the right price list? For flour, a fine above $2 makes no sense, because buying more flour would be cheaper than paying it: \(\max(0,\ y - 2)\).
The gap. The shopper pays \(2x\). The fines tell you the least anyone could ever pay, here \(3y\). At the answer these two numbers match (\(2 \times 3 = 3 \times 2 = 6\)), so the gap \(|2x - 3y|\) should be zero.

Each check is divided by the size of the problem’s numbers, so the score means the same thing for a tiny problem and a huge one. When all three checks are below a small tolerance (by default 0.001), Jaddle stops: it has found the answer.
Write \(m\) for the score now and \(m_{\text{last}}\) for the score at the last restart. Jaddle restarts when one of these happens:

Good progress: \(m \le 0.2\, m_{\text{last}}\). The score has dropped to a fifth. Lock in the gain.
Stalling: \(m \le 0.8\, m_{\text{last}}\) and the score has started going back up. We made some progress and it’s fading, so lock it in.
Taking too long: the current stretch has gone on for a long time without either of the above, so restart anyway.

At a restart Jaddle:

jumps to whichever of the current point or the average has the better score;
updates \(k\), \(t\) and \(s\) using what it saw during the stretch;
throws away the old average and starts a new one;
keeps \(\eta\), since the step it learned is still useful.



See the difference
Here is the flour problem again with a fixed step size, scored by the wrongness score above. The score axis is logarithmic: each step down the axis divides the score by the same amount.

restartData = {
  const merit = (x, y) =&gt; Math.max(
    Math.max(0, 3 - x) / 4,
    Math.max(0, y - 2) / 3,
    Math.abs(2 * x - 3 * y) / (1 + Math.abs(2 * x) + Math.abs(3 * y))
  );
  const step = (x, y, eta) =&gt; {
    x = Math.max(0, x - eta * (2 - y));
    y = Math.max(0, y + eta * (3 - x));
    return [x, y];
  };
  const eta = 0.4, N = 300, floor = 1e-16, rows = [];

  // No restarts: score the current point and the average.
  let x = 0, y = 0, sx = 0, sy = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= N; i++) {
    [x, y] = step(x, y, eta);
    sx += x; sy += y;
    rows.push({i, score: Math.max(floor, merit(x, y)), method: "Current point only"});
    rows.push({i, score: Math.max(floor, merit(sx / i, sy / i)), method: "Average"});
  }

  // With restarts.
  x = 0; y = 0; sx = 0; sy = 0;
  let n = 0, mLast = merit(0, 0), prev = Infinity;
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= N; i++) {
    [x, y] = step(x, y, eta);
    n++; sx += x; sy += y;
    const ax = sx / n, ay = sy / n;
    const ma = merit(ax, ay), mi = merit(x, y), m = Math.min(ma, mi);
    rows.push({i, score: Math.max(floor, m), method: "Average + restarts"});
    const good = m &lt;= 0.2 * mLast;
    const stalling = m &lt;= 0.8 * mLast && m &gt; prev;
    if (n &gt;= 10 && (good || stalling)) {
      if (ma &lt;= mi) { x = ax; y = ay; }
      sx = 0; sy = 0; n = 0; mLast = m; prev = Infinity;
    } else {
      prev = m;
    }
  }
  return rows;
}







Plot.plot({
  width: 620,
  height: 380,
  grid: true,
  x: {label: "turns →"},
  y: {type: "log", label: "↑ wrongness score (log scale)", tickFormat: ".0e"},
  color: {
    legend: true,
    domain: ["Current point only", "Average", "Average + restarts"],
    range: ["#4c78a8", "#f58518", "#54a24b"]
  },
  marks: [
    Plot.line(restartData, {x: "i", y: "score", stroke: "method", strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})






The current point (blue) circles forever and never improves. The average (orange) slowly creeps in. With restarts (green) the score drops off a cliff and reaches the limit of what the computer can store (about 16 digits) in roughly 200 turns.

The takeaway
When a problem is too hard to solve exactly, you don’t have to give up. You can:

Relax it into an easier problem that gives you a ceiling;
Solve the easy version;
Round its answer back into a real solution;
Compare that solution to the ceiling to know exactly how good it is.

This “relax and round” recipe shows up all over computer science, from scheduling to circuit design to clustering data. And it all started with a clever question: what if the needles could point anywhere?
